<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Blue Bus.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Fill in a form with their name and age with adult help. Interactive: Write very simple sentences with assistance, even if errors are present.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can complete a simple form with teacher assistance, filling in details about transportation items (e.g., writing "red" for the color of a bus). Can write simple words independently to label transportation items on drawings (e.g., "bus," "car"). Can write very simple sentences with assistance, even if errors are present (e.g., "This is a green car.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of prior knowledge.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing colorful flashcards of vehicles (bus, car, bike, truck, train, taxi). The teacher says, "What color is this bus?" and encourages students to respond chorally, "It's a blue bus!" Students point to the corresponding vehicle. The teacher uses TPR by having students 'drive' or 'ride' in place when a vehicle is named.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "It is a blue bus." The teacher points to each word as they say it, then highlights the initial sound of "blue" (/b/) and "bus" (/b/). The teacher then models writing the words "blue" and "bus" clearly, demonstrating letter formation for 'b'.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks: "Is this a red bus?" (showing a blue bus flashcard) – students respond "No!" or shake their heads. "What color is this car?" (pointing to a yellow car flashcard) – students say "Yellow!" or "It's yellow!"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays a large, simple picture chart showing various vehicles with their names and colors written underneath (e.g., a "red car," a "green bike," a "yellow taxi," a "purple truck," an "orange train"). The sentences "This is a red car." and "It is a green bike." are also written next to the pictures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing objects: When describing colors in transportation, use phrases that describe vehicles, like "It is a red bike." "That is a green car." or "It's a blue bus." The focus is on recognizing these phrases in written form.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture of a "yellow taxi" on the chart and reads, "It is a yellow taxi." Then points to the written words "yellow taxi." "What is this color?" (eliciting "yellow"). "What is this vehicle?" (eliciting "taxi").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Is this a green car?" pointing to the "green bike" picture and written sentence. Students shake heads "No" and point to the "green bike" sentence. The teacher says, "Please, point to the red car."</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing color + noun phrases (e.g., "red car," "blue bus") and simple descriptive sentences using "It is a [color] [vehicle]." Focusing on initial sounds like /k/ in 'car' and /y/ in 'yellow' during writing.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Tracing & Matching):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of vehicles (e.g., a blue bus, a red car, a green bike) and dotted-line words/phrases next to them. Students trace the words "blue bus," "red car," "green bike" with their pencils. Then, they draw lines to match the traced words to other pictures of the same vehicles on the worksheet.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> The teacher provides another worksheet with incomplete sentences and pictures. Example: "This is a _____ _____." (picture of an orange truck). "It is a _____ _____." (picture of a small yellow train). Students choose words from a provided word bank (e.g., orange, truck, yellow, train, big, small) to fill in the blanks. The teacher guides them to match colors and vehicles.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. After completing their fill-in-the-blanks worksheets, they swap papers with their partner. The teacher models how to say, "Is this a yellow train?" and the partner checks if the words match the picture. They briefly check each other's work for simple correctness of words.</li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Colorful Vehicle" — A drawing and guided writing activity.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a blank sheet of paper and crayons. They are instructed to draw their favorite transportation item (e.g., a car, bus, bike, train) and color it.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">On the board, the teacher writes sentence frames: "This is a _____ _____." and "It is _____." (e.g., for color or size).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">With teacher assistance and modeling, students then write two simple sentences below their drawing, using the provided frames. For example, if a student drew a big red bus, they would write: "This is a red bus. It is big." The teacher circulates, offering individual help with spelling words like 'car', 'bus', 'red', 'green', and encouraging legible writing.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity directly connects to the Theme "That's a Blue Bus." and Scenario "Colors of Things that Go" by having students produce written descriptions of colored vehicles.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategies and student reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Colorful Vehicle" writing activity. The checklist includes items like: "Identified vehicle name (e.g., bus, car)," "Identified color name (e.g., red, blue)," "Attempted to write a simple sentence (e.g., This is a red car.)."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Colorful Vehicle" drawing with their two written sentences.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible writing of at least one vehicle name (car, bus, bike).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible writing of at least one color name (green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to write at least one simple sentence using the target grammar "Color + noun" (e.g., "This is a green car.") with teacher assistance.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing or circle activity: "Did I write a color word today?" (Yes / No). "Did I write a vehicle word today?" (Yes / No).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did I write today about vehicles or colors?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or hard to write a sentence like "This is a red car."? Why?</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing practice worked well for most students (e.g., tracing, fill-in-the-blanks)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with writing simple words or sentences? (e.g., more direct letter formation practice, individual word cards).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students demonstrate phonemic awareness of initial sounds like /k/ in "car" or /b/ in "bus" when attempting to write the words?</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5, students will integrate their listening, reading, speaking, and writing skills to complete a collaborative project, possibly creating a class "transportation book" using their drawings and sentences from today.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>